A||||6||mbramble||Wiring Question||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||03-31-2001||11:50 AM||||In my 87 coupe, as well as the 85 SE I just spent time removing stuff from at the junkyard, there is a set of wires coming out of the steering wheel column.  Almost like a flat ribbon cable that ends in a long connector.  There is a matching long connector that goes into one of the wiring harnesses.  What is this for???  My newly acquired 85 GT does not have this and I'm wondering if this is just one of the many wiring "mods" that have obviously made to this car -- or if it's not supposed to be there on this particular model (mine is a 4 spd with cruise control).<P>I think getting the wiring right is going to be the most time consuming part of getting this thing back on the road.  Lots of changes to wiring in the front of the car....<P>Also, is there a diagram anywhere that shows the routing of wires and wiring harnesses??  Not pin outs cause they don't help a lot in trying to physically identify what a particular bundle of wires is for.....<BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 03-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||03-31-2001||12:03 PM||||That long connector should be in all Fiero's. It comes from the turn signal switch unit behind the steering wheel.<BR>The best place I've found for harness routing pictures is the Helm factory service manual.  The one for my 84 has several pages showing harness routing and connector location.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||03-31-2001||12:15 PM||||Somehow I <I>knew</I> you were gonna say that!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Guess I'm gonna have to go back and get that wiring as well.  Looks as though the steering wheel will need to be pulled.<P>Is there a source for the factory service manual -- besides the occassional one on e-bay??<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||03-31-2001||12:25 PM||||Try either Rodney Dickman or The Fiero Store.  Ebay may be cheaper, but yoiu have to take what you can find.  I'll get getting one in the next few weeks.  They Haynes manual just doesn't cut it....||||reg||14
Z||000004||MrPBody||03-31-2001||02:23 PM||||I think you can still get them from Helm ( <A HREF="http://www.helminc.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.helminc.com/</A> ) for $75.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mbramble||03-31-2001||06:36 PM||||Fortunately, it looks as though I was wrong about these wires being pulled out.  Yea!!  In my coupe and the SE at the junkyard, the harness hangs down and is clipped to the lower instrument panel cover.  The wires in this GT have been wrapped around the steering column.  But they are there...<P>Does anyone have an extra plug that goes in the back of the stereo??  That is definitely cut off and has been cut off on the car at the boneyard.  I have acquired two GM radio/cassettes.  One, which I am pretty sure would be the stock Fiero unit; the 12  pins in it are flat.  The other, I believe is newer and has the equalizer; the pins are thin and round like small nails and there are many more than 12.  I would like to get a connector for each of these (and I need a pinout for the newer one) so that I can test them out.  <P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phaeton||04-01-2001||05:13 AM||||The Pontiac dealer should have the connectors available, both the twelve pin original connector and the 12 to 21 pin adaptor to put later model stereo in the Fiero.||||reg||12
